ok - report mode lists every copy with class and reason, changes nothing, and repeats exactly
ok - live nested copies are named once with lock/directory ages, below budget, without changing copies or locks
ok - a lock probe cut by the time bound names the copy with an unknown source and age, never its directory age
ok - a registered copy whose directory is gone is not named as a live nested copy
ok - the wake carries the count, the oldest five live copies and a pointer to the report, as one line
ok - a new youngest copy is named first, the wake names five copies in all, and an unchanged set stays silent
ok - home-project nested copies are reported while their task is live, but not after closure or for a secondmate home
ok - unreadable registered-worktree enumeration reports the gap without a directory-walk fallback
ok - apply removes only the REAPABLE-PROVEN copy; dirty, unpushed, live, orphan, locked and in-use copies stay byte-identical; a rerun is idempotent
ok - a live recycled launcher pid holds its copy and unchanged lock, with age and lock mtime reported
ok - a dead-launcher locked copy with untracked work stays registered and locked, without a driver call or sweep data
ok - a dead-launcher locked copy with a process inside stays registered and locked, without a driver call or sweep data
ok - a locked copy whose launcher pid has finished is planned, preserved, released by the driver and removed, one run, with the net GiB freed reported
ok - an exact env_loading missing-.env warning on stderr does not veto an allowed stdout plan, and the cap still limits executes
ok - missing-.env stderr warnings remain visible but do not turn either temporary stdout refusal into a permanent receipt
ok - a failed nested-registration enumeration holds a clean locked copy before any driver call
ok - a failed post-removal registration proof reports unproved, keeps the receipt, and never reports or logs REMOVED
ok - the default cap releases five of seven locked copies and defers two unchanged until the next run; an explicit cap of two behaves the same way
ok - a refused plan, a failed preservation, a failed reproof and temporary driver refusals use no release slot, so five copies that refuse on every run cannot starve the clean copies behind them: one run releases five clean copies and defers two, and the next run releases those two
ok - a post-execute enumeration that prints the candidate and then fails is unknown, uses a release slot, and limits seven untouched driver refusals to five executes with two deferred
ok - a driver execute that reports success but leaves the copy registered, or fails after releasing its lock, uses a release slot, so a systematic partial release reaches at most five copies per run and defers the rest
ok - a dead-launcher UUID-owner creation lock uses the same supported driver release
ok - a dead-launcher creation lock with an owner=ppid:<pid> owner uses the same driver release, because only its launcher pid and the other proofs decide
ok - exit-3 ignored-payload retention is KEPT, keeps its lock and source, and repeated sweeps neither preserve nor execute again
ok - a task record appearing during the driver plan prevents preservation and release
ok - a different but valid Vernant lock appearing during the plan is never released
ok - extra lock lines stay byte-identical and never reach the driver
ok - NUL bytes cannot normalize an unsupported lock into an allowed creation lock
ok - a driver that releases its lock but fails removal leaves a durable protection against later plain removal
ok - a retention receipt whose copy is gone or whose path now holds a different copy, locked or not, is ignored by classification, left by report, and removed and listed by apply, so a new copy at that path is judged on its own proofs
ok - report distinguishes a local-proof candidate from a driver-approved removal without executing the driver
ok - only a boolean positive verdict with exit zero is permission; invalid types, documents, suffixes and nonzero exit keep lock bytes unchanged
ok - a copy whose plan the driver refuses is held with the driver's reason, nothing is copied, the run exits 0, and the next run releases it once the plan allows it
ok - a plan that reports the driver's retain rule holds the copy with the driver's words, copies nothing, never executes, and exits 0
ok - a failure after an allowed plan leaves the copy locked and registered, the run exits 1, and the FAILED line carries the driver's refusal lines and exit code
ok - an execute refusal the driver names temporary (lease conflict, live or unknown tenant) that released nothing leaves no receipt and discards its own run's preserve, so repeated refusals add no preserve while the copy keeps its originals, and the next allowed run preserves once and releases the copy; an original that changed in the copy during preservation stops the release before execute, keeps the preserve of the old content and the next run still retries the copy; an original that changed during execute before the same temporary refusal keeps that preserve with no receipt and the next quiet run releases the copy; the same refusal after an unlock stays held with its preserve
ok - a copy whose preserved content changed after its preservation reports WAITING, never HELD, with no new preserve and no driver call while its content keeps changing across runs; the first run that sees it unchanged preserves once and releases it through the driver, and no preserve is lost; content that changes back to its latest preserve is released without a duplicate preserve
ok - ignored content that changes after the last proof, after a preserve was built or an earlier preserve was reused, stops the release before the driver's execute, uses no release slot and keeps every preserved byte, and a later quiet run preserves the new content once and releases the copy
ok - a new ignored file that appears after preservation, with or without an earlier preservable file, stops the release before execute with no release slot and no lost bytes, records the copy's content, so the next run that still sees it changing reports WAITING with no preserve and no driver call, and the first quiet run preserves once and releases the copy
ok - a preservable ignored directory holding a FIFO and a stale socket is compared as preservation copies it: temporary refusals discard each run's preserve with no waiting record, so preserves never accumulate, and the allowed run preserves once and releases the copy
ok - a driver-retention receipt that cannot be written before execute stops the release, discards that run's preserve while the copy still holds its lock and originals, so repeated refusals add no preserve, and the next run that can write it preserves once and releases the copy
ok - a driver answer whose sorted keys put its reason past 600 characters still shows that reason first in the plan hold, the KEPT or FAILED line, the receipt and the later held reason
ok - a copy whose ignored files cannot be preserved stays locked and registered, the sweep's own FAILED line ends in a human-only question with no command and no friction event, and the run exits 1
ok - a failed preservation removes the staging directory it built, so repeated failed sweeps leave no staging bytes behind
ok - a copy whose creation lock became someone else's after its plan stays locked, is not released by the driver, and is reported as the sweep's own refusal
ok - a task record or a different valid lock appearing during preservation stops the release after the copy, with no execute and no receipt; this run's preserve is discarded only while the copy keeps the plan's lock and every preserved original unchanged, and a later quiet run whose content equals that kept preserve releases the copy through the driver without preserving it again
ok - an empty, malformed, missing, false-with-text or garbage-literal plan verdict and a plan that never answers all hold the copy locked, copy nothing, run the driver only in plan mode, leave no driver process running, and exit 0
ok - a plan whose whole answer is the bare text allowed=true holds the copy and never executes the driver
ok - a zero-padded plan bound is decimal: 08 holds a hung plan at 8s and 08 or 09 releases a copy whose plan allows it
ok - a sweep stopped while its plan hangs stops the driver and its child too
ok - new copies at the same path and HEAD retain both versions of their preserved ignored bytes
ok - a removal whose size was not measured shows - and unmeasured, and the closing total counts it
ok - a locked copy with an unpushed but content-equivalent commit is held, and the driver never runs
ok - a live launcher, a lock that is not Vernant's, and a missing driver all keep their copy untouched
ok - a copy with one untracked file and a copy whose tip is on no remote are both kept
ok - a copy whose tip is on no remote but whose content already landed is removed by the teardown landed rule
ok - a project clone under a Firstmate home is enumerated, budgeted as a container, and swept
ok - a failing git worktree remove leaves the copy, is reported, exits 1, and is attempted once
ok - apply never forces, cleans, unlocks, recursively deletes or calls treehouse
